I don't think that should be the case, as we can have a host directive lets again say [icon] that have required input which we will set in programmatically for example in constructor. For example 

```
@Directive({
  selector: '[icon]',
  standalone: true,
})
export class IconDirective {
  @Input({ required: true }) icon: string;
}

@Directive({
  selector: '[closeIcon]',
  hostDirectives: [IconDirective],
})
export class CloseIconDirective {
  iconDirective = inject(IconDirective);

  constructor() {
    this.iconDirective.icon = 'close';
  }
}
```

@kbrilla 
This could be a solution:

```javascript
// Use this one as a host directive
@Directive({
  standalone: true,
})
export class IconDirective {
  @Input() iconName: string;
}

// Use this one in templates
@Directive({
  selector: '[icon]',
  standalone: true,
  inputs: [{name: 'iconName', required: true}]
})
export class IconDirectiveForTemplates extends IconDirective {}
```
